    ],
    "proof_tex": "By \\(\\text{def:criterion}\\), the criterion equals one precisely when either\n\\(J_+(S_k)=\\varnothing\\), or \\(J_+(S_k)\\ne\\varnothing\\) and\n\\(\\mathcal H_\\star(B)\\ne\\varnothing\\).  In either case the corresponding affirmative branch of \\(\\text{thm:local-single-hazard-alternative}\\) gives, for every compatible law,\n\\[\nQ(P)=\\mathsf{Rec}^{(1,\\mathrm{loc})}_{T,k,S_k,B}((M_d(P))_{d=0}^T).\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nSuppose two laws \\(P,P'\\in\\mathcal P_{T,S,B,e}\\) have the same observed law.  By \\(\\text{prop:audit-table-equivalence}\\), their pattern tables agree.  Applying the same deterministic map in (22) gives\n\\[\nQ(P)=\\mathsf{Rec}^{(1,\\mathrm{loc})}_{T,k,S_k,B}((M_d(P))_d)\n=\\mathsf{Rec}^{(1,\\mathrm{loc})}_{T,k,S_k,B}((M_d(P'))_d)=Q(P').\n\\]\nThis is exactly \\(\\operatorname{GID}(T,S,B)=1\\) by\n\\(\\text{def:graphwise-identification}\\).\n\nConversely, if the criterion equals zero, then necessarily\n\\(J_+(S_k)\\ne\\varnothing\\) and \\(\\mathcal H_\\star(B)=\\varnothing\\).  The negative branch of \\(\\text{thm:local-single-hazard-alternative}\\) supplies\n\\(P,P'\\in\\mathcal P_{T,S,B,e}\\) with equal observed laws and unequal endpoint intervention vectors.  This pair falsifies the implication in\n\\(\\text{def:graphwise-identification}\\), so \\(\\operatorname{GID}(T,S,B)=0\\).  The alternative proves all coordinates simultaneously and its calculations use only positive scalar normalization and Markov factorization, never transition-matrix inversion.  Hence the conclusion includes deficient ranks and null allowed-arrow effects.",
    "justification": "The iff theorem proves that the finite graph-and-incidence rule is both sufficient and necessary for the entire causal vector.",
    "gap": "No cited result supplies this complete partial-audit criterion for the arbitrary-horizon at-most-one-informative-hazard Markov family.",
    "consumer": "The theorem decides whether a planned partial recontact identifies every treatment-specific endpoint probability.",
